The `catch (Exception e)` is quite broad. It will silently swallow programming errors such as `NullPointerException` or `IllegalStateException` that indicate a bug, logging them only at DEBUG level. Consider catching the specific expected exceptions (`IndexNotFoundException`, `ShardNotFoundException`, `AlreadyClosedException`) and letting unexpected ones propagate, or at minimum log unexpected exceptions at WARN/ERROR level. For example:
```java
} catch (IndexNotFoundException | ShardNotFoundException | AlreadyClosedException e) {
    logger.atDebug().withThrowable(e).log("Failed to get checkpoint for shard [{}] and index [{}]", shardId.getId(), shardId.getIndexName());
    listener.onFailure(e);
    return;
}
```
This way, a genuine `NullPointerException` from a coding mistake would still surface as an unhandled exception rather than being masked behind a debug log line.

